'''Jala''' ({{lang|ca|Jala}}, from Volisanian ''jala'' "highland, upland") is the second most populous [[Provinces of Balboa|province]] of [[Balboa]], occupying the central highlands of the [[Balboa isthmus]] from the [[Shendan Ocean]] on the western coast to the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] on the eastern coast. It is bordered by [[Tramuntana Province]] to the northwest and [[Nurra Province]] to the northeast, with the [[Yalabirga Mountain Range]] dividing the two, and by [[Estret Province]] to the south. The province has an area of 65,977 km² and a population of approximately 8.5 million (2026 estimate). The provincial capital is [[Sant Bart]], which served as the national capital during the rump state period (1899–1973) and remains the principal administrative centre of Balboa.

Jala is the industrial heartland of Balboa's domestic processing chain. The [[Yalabirga Mountain Range]] runs through the province from northwest to southeast, reaching elevations of 1,200 to 1,800 metres in the central massif and forming the watershed divide between the Shendan (western) and Aethiopian (eastern) drainage basins. The [[Badia Curta]] industrial complex on the Shendan coast is the centre of the country's petroleum refining and chemical manufacturing industries, and the Yalabirga foothills contain bauxite and copper mines that feed domestic smelters. The province accounts for approximately 18 per cent of national GDP.

== Geography ==

[[File:Yalabirga cloud forest.png|thumb|right|300px|Cloud forest in the Yalabirga highlands above the Sant Bart valley.]]

Jala spans the width of the Balboa isthmus, from the [[Shendan Ocean]] on the western coast to the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] on the eastern coast, with the [[Yalabirga Mountain Range]] running through the centre of the province from northwest to southeast. The range enters the province from the northwest, where it divides [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] from [[Nurra Province|Nurra]], and runs southeast through the central highlands before subsiding into the lowlands of [[Estret Province|Estret]] to the south. The Yalabirga forms the watershed divide of the isthmus: rivers on the eastern slope flow eastward to the [[Aethiopian Ocean]], while rivers on the western slope flow westward to the [[Shendan Ocean]].

The principal rivers of the eastern slope include the [[Riu Noguera]], which flows through the central highlands and the Sant Bart valley before descending to the Aethiopian plain; the [[Riu Gaià]], which drains the Costabella region; the [[Riu Montsant]], which flows through the Bosc Verd highlands; and the [[Riu Siurana]], which drains the southern comarcas toward the Aethiopian coast. On the western slope, the [[Riu Cardener]] flows northwest through the Badia Curta area, the [[Riu Anoia]] drains the central western comarcas, and the [[Riu Foix]] and [[Riu Tordera]] flow westward through the southern highlands to the Shendan coast.

The [[Estany de Llacuna]], a natural lake in the southern comarca of [[Llacuna (comarca)|Llacuna]], is the province's principal inland body of water and a centre of resort tourism. The [[Illa Roja]], a small island of approximately 25 km² in the Aethiopian waters off the eastern coast, is administered as a separate comarca and is the province's only island territory.

The province's topography ranges from the highland valleys of the central massif, where the principal cities are located, to the coastal plains on both the Shendan and Aethiopian coasts. The Aethiopian (eastern) coastal plain is broader and more developed, descending gradually from the highlands to the coast; it includes the province's second-largest city, [[Costabella]], and the corridor followed by [[Expressway 3 (Balboa)|Expressway 3]]. The Shendan (western) coastal plain is narrower and drier, lying in the rain shadow of the Yalabirga. The western comarcas are more rugged and sparsely populated, characterised by steep terrain and extensive forest cover.

=== Climate ===

Jala's climate varies with elevation and exposure. The central highlands, at elevations of 1,200 to 1,800 metres, experience a {{w|tropical highland climate}} with average annual temperatures of 18–24 °C, significantly cooler than the coastal provinces. The Aethiopian (eastern) coastal plain is hot and humid, with average temperatures of 26–29 °C and annual rainfall of 1,500–2,500 mm. The Shendan (western) coastal plain, in the rain shadow of the Yalabirga, is drier, with average temperatures of 26–29 °C and annual rainfall of 800–1,200 mm.

The province experiences a wet season from May to December and a dry season from January to April. The highest precipitation occurs in the {{w|cloud forest}} zone of the central massif, where {{w|orographic lift}} produces persistent fog and heavy rainfall. The eastern Aethiopian coast is subject to seasonal tropical storms, particularly between August and October, while the Shendan coast is largely protected by the mountain barrier.

=== Flora and fauna ===

{{main|Wildlife of Balboa}}

The Yalabirga highlands support {{w|cloud forest}} ecosystems, characterised by persistent fog, high humidity, and dense vegetation including epiphytes, mosses, and tree ferns. The cloud forests of the central massif are among the most extensive on the isthmus and are a significant attraction for eco-tourism and hiking. The lower slopes support {{w|tropical moist forest}}, transitioning to {{w|tropical dry forest}} on the Shendan (western) slope, which lies in the rain shadow of the range.

The highland streams of the Yalabirga were historically a source of gold, extracted by the [[Birú people|Birú]] in the pre-colonial period and later by Volisanian colonial operations. Small-scale gold mining persists in the remote highland districts, though the sector contributes less than 1 per cent of provincial GDP and employs only a few thousand workers.

The [[Birú people|indigenous Birú]] population is concentrated in the remote highland comarcas of Jala and [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]]. The Birú retreated into the highlands following the Volisanian conquest and have maintained communities in relative isolation. The [[Birú Dale language]], the Birú's ancestral tongue, is critically endangered; most Birú under the age of fifty speak Volisanian or [[Balboan Creole]] as their first language.

